    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d252">

                <pb facs="0485" n="411" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg411"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.7441/39</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Minister of Supply</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Beaverbrook</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Presidents Special Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-03">January 3,
                        1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Harry</hi>: You spoke to me the other day
                    about direct Cable and Wireless communication between the United States and
                    British Empire countries. You asked that direct communication should be
                    permitted for Government messages for the duration of the war and I have now
                    received a report<anchor n="1" xml:id="d252-f1"/> on the position from Sir
                    Walter Layton.</p>
                <p>The Advisory Committee on Imperial Communications is meeting next week and I am
                    putting forward the request for direct communications for Government messages
                    during the war.</p>
                <p>It is expected that the Committee will consent to your plan.</p>
                <p>Yours ever,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Max</hi>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#small-caps">Lord Beaverbrook</hi>] </signed><anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d252-f2"/>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
